---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import CalloutPanel from '../components/CalloutPanel.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Take action';
const pageDescription =
  'Practical steps: how to talk with expecting parents, what to ask a pediatrician, the exact language for a birth plan, and how to prevent forced retraction at checkups.';

const pediatricianQuestions = [
  'Do you follow the AAP guidance that an intact foreskin should never be forcibly retracted?',
  'How many intact patients does your practice care for?',
  'Will you note "intact — do not retract" in his chart?',
  'If a foreskin concern ever comes up, what would you try before recommending surgery?',
];
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} pageKey="take-action">
  <section class="section section--tight action-head">
    <div class="container container--reading">
      <h1>Take action</h1>
      <p class="action-head__lede">
        Most circumcisions happen because nobody in the room questioned the
        default. Almost everything below is a way of asking the question out
        loud, calmly, before the moment it matters.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading action-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Before the birth"
        heading="Talking with expecting parents"
      />
      <p>
        You will rarely change a mind by winning an argument. What works is
        planting one question early: <em>"Have you looked into whether it's
        actually recommended? It surprised me."</em> Lead with questions, not
        verdicts. Share one link — the <a href="/evidence">evidence page</a>
        or a single care guide — rather than ten.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be gentle with circumcised fathers, including the ones doing the
        deciding. Most were infants when it was done to them and are hearing
        all of this for the first time while being asked to conclude
        something uncomfortable about their own bodies. The point is never
        blame; it is that their son can simply be left whole. One calm
        conversation before the third trimester outweighs any number of
        debates in the delivery ward.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading action-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Choosing care"
        heading="Questions for a pediatrician"
        lede="You are interviewing for someone who will examine your son dozens of times. These four questions tell you most of what you need to know:"
      />
      <ul class="check-list">
        {pediatricianQuestions.map((q) => (
          <li>
            <Icon name="check" size={16} class="check-list__icon" />
            <span>{q}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Hesitation on the first question is disqualifying — non-retraction
        has been standard guidance for decades. The rest is about comfort and
        experience with intact anatomy, which prevents unnecessary
        "treatment" of a normal, still-fused foreskin.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading action-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="At the hospital"
        heading="Birth-plan and hospital language"
        lede="Circumcision requires your explicit, signed consent. You do not need to argue or justify — you only need to decline, clearly, to every shift. Copy this into your birth plan:"
      />
      <blockquote class="birth-plan">
        Our son is to remain intact. Do not circumcise him. Do not retract
        his foreskin for cleaning, examination, or catheter placement —
        discuss alternatives with us first. We do not consent to any
        procedure on his genitals.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Practical notes: keep the baby with a parent whenever possible; state
        the plan verbally at intake and at each nursing shift change; and know
        that "it's hospital routine" is not a consent form. If a consent
        document for circumcision appears in your paperwork stack, decline to
        sign it — nothing happens without it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading action-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Every checkup"
        heading="Protecting an intact child at the doctor's office"
      />
      <p>
        The main ongoing risk to an intact boy in the United States is not
        disease — it is a clinician who was never taught intact anatomy
        retracting the foreskin during an exam. The phrase to know is
        <strong>forced retraction</strong>: it causes tearing, scarring, and
        the very problems it gets blamed on, and it contradicts the AAP's own
        guidance.
      </p>
      <p>
        Say "he's intact — no retraction" at the start of any genital exam,
        the same way you'd mention an allergy. You may decline any part of an
        exam, and you may physically stay beside your child during all of it.
        If a clinician insists retraction is needed for cleaning, that is a
        sign to find a different clinician — the <a href="/evidence">care
        section of the evidence page</a> has the guidance to point to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading action-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Beyond your family"
        heading="Support the wider effort"
      />
      <p>
        The organizations doing year-round education and advocacy run on
        donations and volunteers. The <a href="/resources">resources page</a>
        lists them with a note on what each one does — pick the style that
        fits you, from printable care guides to physician-led advocacy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CalloutPanel
    heading="The evidence behind all of this"
    body="Every claim on this page traces back to the sources collected on the evidence page — medical-organization positions, the research on benefits and risks, and the care guidance."
    primaryLabel="Read the evidence"
    primaryHref="/evidence"
    secondaryLabel="Browse resources"
    secondaryHref="/resources"
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  .action-head {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .action-head h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .action-head__lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .action-prose p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .check-list {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .check-list li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .check-list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .check-list :global(.check-list__icon) {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    align-self: center;
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }

  .birth-plan {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-teal);
    background-color: var(--color-mist);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.7;
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
  }
</style>
